---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Block AI Crawlers (robots.txt, WAF, and DNS)"
  description="How to block AI crawlers: the exact robots.txt rules for GPTBot, ClaudeBot, Google-Extended and friends, plus WAF-level blocking for bots that ignore robots.txt."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Block AI Crawlers</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Block AI Crawlers</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      Keeping AI systems off your content is a three-layer decision: robots.txt for the polite ones,
      WAF rules for the ones that misbehave, and legal/technical walls for everything else. Here is
      each layer, exactly.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>Layer 1: robots.txt (works for compliant bots)</h2>
      <p>Drop this block into your robots.txt to refuse the major AI training crawlers:</p>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: OAI-SearchBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Google-Extended
Disallow: /

User-agent: PerplexityBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: CCBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Bytespider
Disallow: /

User-agent: Applebot-Extended
Disallow: /

User-agent: meta-externalagent
Disallow: /</pre>
      <p>
        Note what is deliberately absent: Googlebot. Blocking it removes you from Google Search and
        AI Overviews alike, which almost no site wants. Verify your rules with the
        <a href="/ai-crawler-checker/">AI Crawler Checker</a> after saving.
      </p>

      <h2>Layer 2: WAF and network rules (for bots that ignore robots.txt)</h2>
      <p>
        robots.txt is a convention, not a fence. Bots with poor compliance reputations (Bytespider
        historically among them) may fetch anyway. Cloudflare customers can enable managed AI-bot
        rules or write a firewall rule on the user-agent string; any WAF can match
        <code>User-Agent contains "Bytespider"</code> and issue a challenge or block. This layer
        actually enforces.
      </p>

      <h2>Layer 3: authentication and paywalls</h2>
      <p>
        Content behind a login is not crawled, period. If your model is subscription content, the
        reliable block is authentication plus rate limiting, with robots rules as the courtesy layer
        on top.
      </p>

      <h2>Before you block everything</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Blocking training crawlers does not remove you from search-style answers unless you also block their search bots (OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot).</li>
        <li>Aggressive wildcard Disallow rules often take out legitimate crawlers with them; test changes with the <a href="/robots-txt-checker/">robots.txt checker</a>.</li>
        <li>Vendors also run opt-out portals (OpenAI, Google) that operate independently of robots.txt.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/how-to-allow-ai-crawlers/">How to allow AI crawlers</a> (the other direction)</li>
        <li><a href="/ai-crawlers-list/">The complete list of AI crawlers</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
